<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-phenylalanine biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3462" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME:PHE Phenylalanine| is one of the three proteinogenic aromatic amino acids that are produced only in plants and microorganisms. It is a central metabolite, and plants direct up to 30% of the carbon flux from photosynthesis towards its biosynthesis |CITS:[22554242]|. Moreover, |FRAME:PHE| is precursor of a plethora of secondary metabolites involved in plant defense (e.g. flavonoids |CITS:[11743081][22404468]|), signaling and protection (e.g. isoflavonoids |CITS:[12644640][20921162]|), reproduction (e.g. phenylpropanoids and volatiles |CITS:[20035037]|) and general plant fitness (e.g. vitamin E, plastoquinone, and suberin). The biosynthesis of |FRAME:PHE| has been known to comprise two major pathways, a plant pathway via |FRAME:CPD-659|, which has been claimed to occur exclusively in plastids (|FRAME: PWY-3462|) and the microbial pathway via |FRAME:PHENYL-PYRUVATE| (|FRAME: PHESYN|). The phenylpyruvate route has been confirmed in many microorganisms, including |FRAME: TAX-562| and yeast, and the two routes have been reported co-exist in many microorganisms. However, compelling evidence suggests that many plants employ only the arogenate route |CITS: [3985619][3463961]|. So far the evidence for the phenylpyruvate route in plants is lacking, as |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.51|, has never been detected in any plant (for a conflicting view, see |FRAME: PWY-7432|). |FRAME: CPD-659| is the branch-point and the immediate precursor for the synthesis of both |FRAME: TYR| and |FRAME: PHE|. |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.91|, is the key enzyme channeling |FRAME: CPD-659| towards |FRAME: PHE| synthesis |CITS: [3124763]|. Its activity is strongly feedback inhibited by the latter.</Description>
